<TopicPage slug="subjects/journalism/advanced/data-journalism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Data journalism is the <strong>data-science ladder aimed at power</strong> — acquiring,
    interrogating and publishing the numbers behind the news. It inherits every method lesson
    from the data ladder and every verification lesson from this one.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the pipeline, FOIA, honest analysis, evidentiary graphics" widget="equationStep" config={dataJCfg} />

  <h2>The canon and the craft</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Moneyball for accountability:</strong> the Panama/Paradise Papers (ICIJ, 2016–17): 11 million documents, 400 journalists, structured collaboration — data journalism at global scale: the sharing infrastructure (the distributed-systems lesson\'s tools, editorially governed).</li>
    <li><strong>When data journalism breaks the wrong way:</strong> the COVID infection-model misreadings, crime-mapping that stigmatised neighbourhoods (the ethics lesson\'s minimising harm, quantitative edition) — the discipline\'s error bars must include social consequence.</li>
    <li><strong>Reproducibility is a scoop-protection:</strong> publish the analysis notebooks (GitHub for the methods section) — the computational-reproducibility lesson, newsroom edition; an analysis nobody can check is a claim, not a finding.</li>
    <li><strong>The skills stack, honestly:</strong> spreadsheets first (80% of data journalism), SQL and R/Python for scale, the GIS lesson\'s mapping for spatial stories — the data-science ladder\'s path, deadline-compressed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the data-science and statistics ladders; ICIJ methodology pages; the Cairo chart-honesty lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
